|~ What is declared under `::boolean`: the refusal of its
   constructor, the negation, and the two combinators of conditions,
   which take booleans. ~|

:boolean/PayloadNotBooleanError
  |~~ `::boolean` reads its payload as the boolean it is; a payload of
      another kind is refused.
      ~(
        ::boolean(1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::boolean/PayloadNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:boolean/not
  |~~ The negation of a boolean. Null is tested with `eq null`, and a
      subject of another kind is refused.

      ~(false | not | eq true)
      ~(true | not | eq false)
      ~(null | eq null | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:boolean/NotSubjectNotBooleanError
  |~~ `not` turns a boolean; a value is asked whether it is absent by
      `eq null`, and a container whether it is empty by `empty`.
      ~(
        0 | not
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::boolean/NotSubjectNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:boolean/and
  |~~ Whether the subject and the right condition both hold, each a
      boolean. Two conditions of one record hold together by `allOf`,
      `allOf /active (/age | gte 18)`, which composes the clauses of a
      predicate inside `filter`, `every` and `if`.

      ~(true | and false | eq false)
      ~({:active true :age 25} | allOf /active (/age | gte 18) | eq true)
      ~({:active false :age 25} | allOf /active (/age | gte 18) | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:right ::boolean | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:boolean/AndLeftNotBooleanError
  |~~ The left condition of `and` must answer a boolean.
      ~(
        1 | and true
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::boolean/AndLeftNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:boolean/AndRightNotBooleanError
  |~~ The right condition of `and` must answer a boolean.
      ~(
        true | and 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::boolean/AndRightNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:boolean/or
  |~~ Whether the subject or the right condition holds, each a boolean.
      Conditions of one subject hold by one of them through `anyOf`,
      `5 | anyOf (eq 5) (eq 10)`.

      ~(false | or true | eq true)
      ~(5  | anyOf (eq 5) (eq 10) | eq true)
      ~(7  | anyOf (eq 5) (eq 10) | eq false)
      ~({:role "admin"} | anyOf (/role | eq "admin") (/role | eq "owner") | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:right ::boolean | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:boolean/OrLeftNotBooleanError
  |~~ The left condition of `or` must answer a boolean.
      ~(
        1 | or true
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::boolean/OrLeftNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:boolean/OrRightNotBooleanError
  |~~ The right condition of `or` must answer a boolean.
      ~(
        false | or 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::boolean/OrRightNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()
